The '''Reconstruction of Guajicutea (1824–1853)''' covers the period from the [[Guajicutea-Lacashe War]] through the outbreak of the [[War of Liberation]]. Following the [[Virtuous Revolution]] on 12 July 1824, [[Lacashe]] invaded Guajicutea on 15 August 1824, establishing a six-month occupation that retained the race codes of the previous [[Northern Confederation]] occupation. The Lacashe occupation was overthrown with the assistance of the [[Sartan Volunteer Army]], and Lacashe withdrew on 15 February 1825. The post-war period, known as the Reconstruction (Sartan: ''Reconstrucción''), saw the restored government formalise the state's institutions, modernise the military, and restart cash crop exports. The [[Second Republic of Guajicutea]] was formally established on 12 October 1828. The period was shaped by a regional anti-slavery movement that isolated Lacashe, the rise of political parties divided between internationalism and isolationism, and an economic depression that shifted the balance between them, culminating in the War of Liberation (1853–1856), in which Guajicutea participated.

== Guajicutea-Lacashe War (1824–1825) ==

The [[Virtuous Revolution]] on 12 July 1824 overthrew the [[Partido Unidad Guajicuteana]] occupation government and restored Guajicutean self-governance. The withdrawal of Northern Confederation troops, prompted by the [[Pastry War]], created a power vacuum in the [[Capuyaquiran Sea]]. [[Lacashe]], which had supported the 1812 Northern Confederation invasion and maintained an alliance with the occupation government, dispatched a fleet to Guajicutea on 15 August 1824.

The Lacashe invasion established a six-month occupation. The Lacashe authorities retained the race codes, miscegenation laws, and antiliberal legislation enacted under the Northern Confederation occupation, maintaining the legal framework that had disenfranchised the Guaji, Bayani, and Kaftian populations. The restored government, led by [[Javier Barirí]], resisted the occupation from the island's interior, drawing on the [[Guarda Revolutionary]]'s jungle infrastructure and the political networks established during the Bloody Decade.

== Sartan Volunteer Army and liberation ==

The restored government secured the assistance of the [[Sartan Volunteer Army]] (Sartan: ''Ejército Voluntario Sartano''), an arrangement of Sartan soldiers who volunteered to support the Guajicutean government against the Lacashe occupation. The Volunteer Army was not an official Sartan military deployment but a facilitated arrangement between the Sartan government and the restored government of Guajicutea. [[Sarta]]'s interest in countering Lacashe's influence in the Capuyaquiran Sea provided the geopolitical basis for the arrangement.

The Sartan Volunteer Army, combined with the Guarda Revolutionary forces under the command of [[Tomás Yukurí]], conducted military operations against the Lacashe occupation forces. The combined forces engaged Lacashe garrison units at [[Waribá]] and [[Tabakurí]]. Lacashe, unable to sustain the occupation against the combined resistance, withdrew on 15 February 1825.

[[Mateo Kurí]], the president arrested during the 1812 invasion and exiled to Sarta in 1813, returned to Guajicutea on 3 March 1825. His return symbolised the restoration of the pre-occupation republic, though he did not resume the presidency.

== Reconstruction (1825–1828) ==

The withdrawal of Lacashe forces in February 1825 initiated the Reconstruction (Sartan: ''Reconstrucción''), a period of political, legal, and economic rebuilding following twelve years of Northern Confederation occupation and the brief Lacashe occupation.

The restored government, initially under military leadership shared between Javier Barirí as head of government, Tomás Yukurí as military commander, and Diego Nasarí as political coordinator, repealed the race codes, miscegenation laws, and antiliberal legislation. The Códigos de Raza, Leyes de Mestizaje, and Leyes Antiliberales were formally abolished, and the universal citizenship provisions of the [[Constitutional Amendment of 1803]] were restored. All residents — Sartan, Guaji, Kaftian, and Bayani — were re-enfranchised.

The reconstruction of the island's infrastructure and economy began. A formalised tax code was enacted, replacing the ad hoc taxation imposed during the occupation period. The Guajicutean army and navy were modernised, incorporating equipment and organisational practices from the Sartan Volunteer Army's experience and re-establishing the [[Astillero Real de Waribá]] as a Guajicutean naval installation. Cash crop exports, disrupted during the occupation, were restarted as international demand recovered.

The restored government transitioned to civilian rule over the first years of the reconstruction period. The [[Asamblea Nacional]], dissolved during the 1812 invasion, was reconstituted. The Guarda Revolutionary was demobilised from a military organisation to a political body, with Yukurí overseeing the integration of Guarda fighters into the reconstituted Guajicutean military and Nasarí transitioning to a role in the civilian government.

== Second Republic and political development (1828–1853) ==

The [[Second Republic of Guajicutea]] was formally established on 12 October 1828, marking the completion of the transition from military to civilian government. The constitutional framework of the 1803 amendment — universal citizenship, the elected Asamblea Nacional, and the directly elected presidency — was restored. The first presidential election was held on 12 June 1829, and Javier Barirí was inaugurated on 1 July 1829 as the first president of the Second Republic. Presidential terms were set at four years with no term limits; representative terms at two years with no term limits.

Barirí served two terms (1829–1837). He retired on 1 July 1837 and died on 1 August 1837, one month after his retirement.

=== Party system ===

The restored government (RG) initially functioned as a unified political movement. On 15 May 1832, the RG split into two parties:

The '''[[Partido del Internacionalismo]]''' (PdI) supported greater integration with the emerging international order and general economic liberalisation. The '''[[Partido de Aislamiento]]''' (PdA) advocated the formation of an {{w|autarky}} and a reduction of Guajicutea's involvement in foreign affairs.

The PdA was initially more popular, reflecting the population's desire for isolation following the trauma of the Northern Confederation occupation and the Bloody Decade. The early reconstruction period was characterised by a reluctance to engage with foreign powers, with the exception of a special relationship with [[Sarta]] maintained as a result of the Sartan Volunteer Army's role in the liberation.

=== Depression and political shift (1834–1840) ===

A small economic depression from 1834 to 1838 affected Guajicutea's recovery. The depression reinforced PdA's isolationist appeal, as the party attributed the economic downturn to international trade dependence. However, the [[Lacashe-Asikyira War]] (1838–1840) created demand for imported goods and demonstrated the economic value of international trade connections. The war shifted public sentiment toward the PdI's platform of economic integration and liberalisation.

In the post-depression period, the PdI gained ascendancy as Guajicutea experienced a growth period driven by exports and trade. However, the PdI became increasingly associated with financial interests and corruption, as the party's leadership became entangled with the commercial networks that benefited from liberalisation.

=== Partido por la Tierra ===

The '''[[Partido por la Tierra]]''' (PpLT) was formed in 1851 as a rural and worker-based party advocating greater working conditions. The PpLT emerged in response to the PdI's corruption and the perception that both the PdI and PdA served elite interests rather than those of the rural and working populations. The PpLT began challenging seats in the Asamblea Nacional, introducing a third force into a political landscape previously dominated by the PdI-PdA division.

== Regional anti-slavery movement ==

The reconstruction period coincided with a regional shift against slavery. Guajicutea's anti-slavery position, established under the [[Patrimonio Diversidad]] government and maintained through the occupation, aligned with a broader movement among Capuyaquiran Sea states. As nations began to boycott Lacashe's slave economy, Guajicutea's restored economy benefited from the redirection of trade away from Lacashe.

[[Tapuya]] banned slavery in 1836, becoming one of the first states in the region to abolish the practice formally. [[Balboa]] banned slavery in 1848. The progressive abolition of slavery among neighbouring states increased economic and diplomatic pressure on Lacashe, whose plantation economy remained dependent on enslaved labour.

== Lacashe-Asikyira War (1838–1840) ==

The [[Lacashe-Asikyira War]] (1838–1840) was a conflict between [[Lacashe]] and [[Asikyira]]. Guajicutea maintained official neutrality throughout the war but secretly supplied goods to Asikyira, using the trade networks and maritime infrastructure re-established during the reconstruction period. The covert support reflected Guajicutea's longstanding opposition to Lacashe's slave economy and its strategic interest in weakening Lacashe's regional position without direct military engagement.

== War of Liberation (1853–1856) ==

The [[War of Liberation]] (1853–1856) was a regional war against [[Lacashe]], fought by an alliance including [[Burawa]], [[Volisania]], and Guajicutea. The war aimed to end Lacashe's slave economy, which had survived as the last slave-holding state in the region despite the progressive abolition by neighbouring states and the boycott movement.

Guajicutea's participation was limited but strategically significant. The restored government dispatched a small troop assignment and granted docking rights to Burawa and Volisania, allowing their fleets to stage an invasion of Lacashe from Guajicutean ports. The use of [[Waribá]] and [[Barikú]] as staging points for the allied fleet drew on the island's longstanding role as a resupply hub in the Capuyaquiran Sea and the naval infrastructure rebuilt during the reconstruction period.

The War of Liberation began in 1853 and continued to 1856, extending beyond the end of the reconstruction period.

== Religion and church-state relations ==

The Restoration established a separation of church and state in 1824. Unlike the earlier Constitutional Amendment of 1784, which had limited church power through expropriation and legal restriction, the 1824 separation was pragmatic and amicable. The state did not attack the church or seize its remaining assets. Instead, the restored government began building parallel secular institutions — civil schools, state registries, and government ceremonies — alongside the existing church structures.

The [[Petran Catholic Church]] in Guajicutea retained its spiritual authority and parish networks. The parish-level clergy had earned credibility during the Northern Confederation occupation, when parish networks served as the infrastructure of the [[Patrimonio Diversidad]] underground and the [[Guarda Revolutionary]] resistance. The institutional hierarchy, which had been re-empowered under the occupation's antiliberal legislation, occupied a more ambiguous position, but the restored government chose reconciliation over purge.

The question of compensating the church for lands expropriated under the 1784 amendment was debated in the [[Asamblea Nacional]] during the early years of the Second Republic. The debate was never resolved, and no compensation was paid. The issue remained a point of tension between the church and the state, though it did not escalate to open conflict.

== Arts and culture ==

The reconstruction period produced two distinct artistic movements: a realist movement that documented the experience of the Bloody Decade directly, and a proto-Symbolist movement that emerged in the 1840s and moved beyond literal depiction into symbolic representation.

=== Realism ===

The realist movement emerged in the immediate post-occupation period. Painters, poets, and writers who had lived through the Bloody Decade produced works documenting the public executions, house burnings, and guerrilla warfare that had defined the occupation. Realist art was immediate and documentary in character, concerned with recording what had happened rather than interpreting it. The movement was rooted in the urban centres of [[Tabakurí]] and [[Waribá]], where artists had access to printing presses, exhibition spaces, and literary circles.

=== Proto-Symbolism ===

In the late 1830s and 1840s, a movement emerged that drew on the symbolic vocabulary of Guaji visual tradition — [[Bikumaku]] glyphs, the three powers (sea, mountain, sky), calendar iconography, and catacomb carvings — and applied it to contemporary painting and poetry. The movement was retrospectively termed proto-Symbolism. Unlike realism, which depicted events directly, proto-Symbolism used inherited Guaji symbols to represent the trauma of the occupation and the experience of reconstruction at a symbolic register.

The movement's central figure was [[Sebastián Biku]] (c. 1808–1862), a painter whose early realist works documented the Bloody Decade before he began incorporating Bikumaku glyphs and the three powers into his canvases in the late 1830s. Biku travelled to [[Sarta]] in 1845, where his work was exhibited and introduced to Sartan intellectual circles. This exhibition marked the transmission of the movement from Guajicutea to [[Illypnia]] via Sarta — a reversal of the usual cultural flow, in which Guajicutea had historically been a receiver of Illypnian influences.

[[Ana Flores]] (c. 1815–1890), a painter of urban Sartan-Guaji heritage, developed a more abstract vocabulary within the movement, using earth-toned palettes and landscapes that carried symbolic weight — the land as witness to the events of the occupation rather than as scenery. Flores remained in Guajicutea and deepened the movement's visual language after Biku's departure for Sarta.

[[Andrés Guma]] (c. 1810–1858), a poet and essayist, articulated the movement's theoretical basis. Guma argued that Guajicutean art should draw from its own symbolic tradition rather than imitate Illypnian forms. His essays circulated in Sarta and gave the movement intellectual credibility abroad.

The proto-Symbolist movement was the first instance of Guajicutean art achieving international influence, spreading from Guajicutea to Sarta and from there to Illypnia during the 1840s and 1850s.

== Demographics ==

=== Population ===

The population of Guajicutea grew from approximately 155,000 in 1824 to approximately 195,000 by 1853. This growth was almost entirely the result of natural increase, as immigration during the reconstruction period remained low. The Sartan colonial arrival cap, abolished with the end of the Sartan colony in 1723, was not reinstated, but no significant migration occurred during the reconstruction period.

The Bloody Decade (1814–1822) under the Northern Confederation occupation caused significant casualties, disproportionately among poor rural populations — primarily Guaji, Bayani, and Kaftian agricultural communities. The race codes enacted under the occupation targeted non-Baliscan and non-Sartan populations, making rural villages in the island's interior the primary targets of reprisals. Natural growth during the twelve-year occupation roughly offset the casualties, leaving the 1824 population close to the 1812 level.

=== Ethnic composition ===

The ethnic composition of Guajicutea remained broadly stable through the reconstruction period, with proportions shifting only marginally:

