[[Waribá]] overtook [[Tabakurí]] as the largest city during the reconstruction period, driven by port activity, the restored [[Astillero Real de Waribá]], and the growth of manufacturing. The slow pace of urbanisation reflected the PdA's initial political dominance and the population's reluctance to engage with the commercial economy following the trauma of the occupation.

== Collective sentiment ==

The reconstruction period was shaped by the collective trauma of the Northern Confederation occupation and the Bloody Decade. A generation that had grown up under foreign rule — with public beheadings in plazas, house burnings as collective punishment, and sustained guerrilla warfare in the island's interior — carried the psychological consequences into the post-occupation period. The immediate succession of the Lacashe invasion (August 1824) after the Virtuous Revolution (July 1824) compounded the experience: liberation lasted one month before another occupation began.

The early reconstruction period was characterised by exhaustion and a desire for isolation. The PdA's popularity in the first post-occupation years reflected not an ideological commitment to autarky but a visceral rejection of foreign involvement. The population's relationship with the outside world was defined by suspicion. The special relationship with Sarta, maintained even by the isolationist PdA, was the sole exception, grounded in the Sartan Volunteer Army's role in the liberation rather than in strategic calculation.

The economic depression of 1834–1838 deepened the sense of betrayal: liberation had not produced prosperity. The shift toward the PdI after the Lacashe-Asikyira War (1838–1840) was driven by economic necessity rather than ideological conversion — the population needed imported goods and trade revenue, not a change of heart about foreign engagement.

By the late 1840s and early 1850s, the mood had shifted. The proto-Symbolist movement in the arts represented a move beyond raw documentation toward processing the trauma at a symbolic level. The formation of the [[Partido por la Tierra]] in 1851 signalled that political energy was moving from the PdI-PdA divide toward questions of class and labour — a sign that the population was beginning to look forward rather than backward. The War of Liberation (1853–1856), fought against Lacashe, the power that had occupied the island in 1824–1825, carried a cathartic quality for a population that had been unable to confront its occupier directly.

